the Metal Gear series has always  delivered complex plots with unexpected  twists and revelations altering your  perception of people and events you  thought you understood the middle Gear  Solid 5 the phantom pain continues this  tradition the majority of its plot  focuses on the events at hand fans of  the series will find their diligence  rewarded in ways that newcomers can't  begin to imagine but such loyalty and  knowledge isn't a prerequisite as big  boss the leader of a private military  group that Diamond Dogs you go behind  enemy lines to carry out recon and  assassination contracts as well as  infiltrate the hideouts of your personal  enemies The Phantom Pain's plot mixes  historical events from the 1980s with a  pinch of James Bond villainy in an  exciting dollop of sci-fi dressing  your mission is twofold build a military  force free from the whims of  narrow-minded world powers and destroy  those who wish to take advantage of  fractured global politics for their own  selfish ideals  there are numerous subplots within and  during your long and extensive journey  you face topics rarely seen in gaming  torture child soldiers and a human cost  of nuclear proliferation  the phantom pain depicts these issues  with unveiled brutality reminding you  that any order we know today came at the  cost of somebody else's freedom and  happiness the prologue middle Gear Solid  5 Ground zeroes gave us a taste of the  series new open-world mechanics which  feel is excellent now as they did in  that game but the freedom of choice in  ground zeroes pales in comparison to the  possibilities that await you in the  phantom pain you don't always have to  sneak in some cases you must attack  head-on both types of scenarios and  still a nerve-racking sense of tension  that either gives way to crushing defeat  or a resounding sense of victory success  isn't just measured by getting from  point A to point B or by defeating a  notorious bad guy remember you're trying  to build a private army while convincing  enemy troops to join your ranks would  require expert coercion in the real  world in the phantom pain you simply  attach a balloon to your friends waist  and they float into the arms of an  awaiting chopper  of course dead soldiers aren't good to  anybody so you're motivated to tread  carefully and quietly to incapacitate  your enemies without raising alarm in  order to capture your prey a lot all of  your hard work capturing soldiers in  addition to resources wildlife and  vehicles pays off back at mother base  your offshore sanctum here soldiers  you've captured can be assigned to  different research fields once your team  hits certain levels and you possess the  right amount of materials that you  source from the field you then earn the  right to develop new equipment there are  dozens of items and pieces of equipment  to unlock and you may spend upwards of a  hundred hours searching for top-notch  recruits and gathering resources while  infiltrating enemy bases if you hope to  unlock everything in the game while  visiting mother base staring out over  the ocean gives you a moment of solace  from the horrible events that surround  you on the battlefield and you get a  similar feeling when riding into battle  seated on the edge of a chopper  reflecting on the meaning behind the  madness at hand the open world is  replete with small military outposts  massive compounds and threats from  nature all opportunities for action and  reward an outpost may contain a vehicle  worth stealing or a soldier that will  provide useful information if you can  quietly detain interrogated unlike  previous Metal Gear games many which  place players in enclosed spaces a  threat can come from miles away and  appear next to you when you least expect  it nothing is confined and no place is  safe you eventually earn the right to  call in a transport via a horse a truck  or even a small robotic walker which is  as charming and expressive as r2d2 from  Star Wars if you play your cards right  you can also take a dog into battle that  will sniff out and distract enemies the  new open-world on the buddy system add  layers of depth that ultimately set the  fan of pain apart from other games in  the series  the story's final strokes cast deep dark  shadows over the world for anyone just  joining the tale of big boss with the  phantom pain the conclusion of these  events will leave you with plenty to  think about everything is questionable  and nothing is as it seems  for fans of the series the ultimate  payoff is one that answers questions but  also one that raises unforeseen  implications unfortunately in the  lead-up to the finale you have to spend  an hour or two replaying older missions  on a higher difficulty setting just to  unlock the final story missions this is  the only aspect of the phantom pain that  feels off ultimately the game delivers  an impactful finale befitting the  journey that preceded it it punches you  in the gut and tears your heart open the  high caliber cutscenes filled with  breathtaking shots and rousing speeches  tease you along the way the story is  dished out in morsels so you'll have to  work for the full meal but it's hard to  call it work when controlling big boss  feels so good with so many possibilities  at your fingertips there has never been  a Metal Gear game with such depth to its  gameplay or so much volume in content  when it comes to storytelling there has  never been a game in this series that's  so consistent in tone daring in subject  matter and so captivating in this  presentation  the phantom pain may be a contender for  one of the best action games ever made  it is undoubtedly the best Metal Gear  game there is for more details on what  you can find in the phantom pain and how  I feel about them check out the full  written review on GameSpot calm  don't even mess it up to you boss make  your way to - uh Google a running to  target  Roger that  we'll pick him up
 

